# Ruby — a cut stone.
#
# The three coloured gems (ruby, sapphire, emerald) share this geometry and
# differ only in the palette, and that is the point: they are the same *kind* of
# object, a faceted stone off a jeweller's wheel, and the colour is the whole
# distinction the player needs. What has to differ is the raw crystals — astral
# and amethyst are grown points, these are cut — so the shape here is the one no
# rough crystal has: a brilliant, pointed pavilion under a wide girdle under a
# flat table.
#
# `shape=box` on six sides is what makes the facets flat panels; a round section
# would make this a spinning top. The girdle is a deliberate double ring 0.06
# apart so there is a hard horizontal break at the widest point — that edge
# catching the key light is the single pixel row that says "cut" rather than
# "pebble" at hotbar size.
#
# The girdle sits at 0.70 of the height and the table is two thirds of its
# width, which is most of what makes this read as a gem the right way up. With
# the widest point at the middle and a narrow top it was a wine glass: the
# pavilion has to be the long half and the crown the short one.

model ruby
  height 0.24
  style chunky

palette
  Ruby  #c72a3c
  Table #ff8a94
  Deep  #7a1220

skeleton
  # The pavilion's point cap hangs below the first ring, so the root sits a
  # little clear of the floor rather than on it — at 0.006 the culet went
  # through the ground plane and the linter said so.
  root culet at 0.034
  bone axis parent=culet dir=up pitch=-3 yaw=8 len=0.80

parts
  loft stone bones=culet..axis material=Ruby sides=6 shape=box
    ring 0.00 w=0.06 d=0.06 material=Deep
    ring 0.20 w=0.30 d=0.30
    ring 0.48 w=0.52 d=0.52
    ring 0.68 w=0.62 d=0.62 material=Ruby
    ring 0.74 w=0.62 d=0.62
    ring 0.90 w=0.46 d=0.46 material=Table
    ring 1.00 w=0.42 d=0.42
    cap start=point end=flat

checks
  assert bottom(stone) < 0.02
  assert bottom(stone) > -0.005
  # A cut stone sits low and broad: the pavilion is under half the height and
  # the girdle is nearly as wide as the whole thing is tall.
  assert height(stone) / width(stone) in 1.1..1.8
  assert parts == 1
  assert tris < 140
